# Grounding layer (Phase B)

Read PROPOSAL §7 before implementing. Do not start this before Phase A ships.

## Span validation — non-negotiable

Every extracted claim carries `verbatim_span`. Validate that the span is an
exact substring of `observation.answer_text`. Reject the claim if it is not.
No fuzzy matching, no normalization, no "close enough."

Without this you get hallucinated claims about hallucinations. This check is
the difference between a real system and a demo.

## Abstain path — mandatory, not an edge case

Five verdict classes: supported, contradicted, unsupported, out_of_scope,
**abstained**.

`abstained` is a first-class outcome, not an error state and not a fallback
to `unsupported`. When label retrieval confidence is below threshold, the
system renders NO verdict and routes to the human queue with the retrieved
candidates attached.

Do not collapse `abstained` into `unsupported`. Do not add a default verdict.
Do not "handle" abstention by picking the most likely class. Abstention rate
is a reported metric, and a high one is a good result, not a bug.

## Section routing

Route each claim_type to its LOINC-coded label section (PROPOSAL §7.2) rather
than searching the whole SPL document. Cache labels locally by setid.

## Retrieval

Implement BM25 first. Implement embeddings second, as a comparison. Report
both in the eval. Do not skip BM25 on the assumption embeddings win — label
language is formulaic and lexical matching may beat dense retrieval here.

## Language

"Flags claims for human review." Never "verifies," "validates," "confirms,"
"checks accuracy," or "fact-checks" — in any string, comment, or docstring.
